WEBVTT


1
00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:08.550
So now let's talk about the actual practical usage of abstract data types in Isabelle.

2
00:00:08.630 --> 00:00:16.040
So what's the point of an abstract data type? It's basically to provide a context in which we can implement an algorithm,

3
00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:25.430
so for implementing generic algorithms, without knowing how a certain data structure is implemented.

4
00:00:25.430 --> 00:00:32.750
We only know that we will have access to that data structure by saying that the algorithm has access to this ADT.

5
00:00:33.790 --> 00:00:40.750
But you don't really care about the precise details about how the abstract data type is implemented.

6
00:00:40.810 --> 00:00:48.000
So you don't, for example, care about how a set is implemented for your function, or how a queue and so on are implemented.

7
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:53.820
All that you care about is that you have an abstract data type, so you have an interface with some logical properties that are specified

8
00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:55.780
to an interface, and that's all that you care about.

9
00:00:56.380 --> 00:01:05.210
And given this context, given this abstract data type, you just define your algorithm and then prove it correct once,

10
00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:12.530
and that proof of correctness once happens within the context, so in Isabelle terms, within the locale,

11
00:01:15.410 --> 00:01:21.930
and then for any implementation of an abstract data type, you don't have to prove your algorithm correctly.

12
00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:28.230
All you need to do is just to say, okay, I'm going to use a 2-3 tree implementation of a set,

13
00:01:28.430 --> 00:01:31.730
or just any other tree implementation of a set,

14
00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:36.390
and then you get, for example, you get your algorithm that uses sets correct, for example.

15
00:01:39.730 --> 00:01:47.210
So an example algorithm that we've talked about, that I want to talk about, is the so-called rem_dups.

16
00:01:47.330 --> 00:01:51.610
So it's a function that removes duplicates from a list.

17
00:01:52.090 --> 00:02:01.570
So here, for instance, you have, for example, if you have a list, say, like this,

18
00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:09.890
so you have a list 1, 2, 1, 5, 2, 1,

19
00:02:09.890 --> 00:02:20.840
you want to remove the duplicate elements from that list, so that rem_dups takes an intuitive level,

20
00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:26.320
it's supposed to be taking that list and removing these duplicate elements from that list,

21
00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:34.070
so you don't want all these 1s and 2s there, you only want one instance of each one of them occurring in the list.

22
00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:46.640
So let's talk about, let's actually see how that

23
00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:49.520
function would be implemented

24
00:02:50.150 --> 00:02:56.050
with the aid of the set abstract data type that we have discussed a moment ago.

25
00:02:56.050 --> 00:03:06.260
So in order for you to know

26
00:03:06.680 --> 00:03:11.980
whether, in order for you to remove duplicates in this way,

27
00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:16.760
you can define the function recursively over the list that you have,

28
00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:19.760
so you have a list and you return a list.

29
00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:25.060
But in addition to that list argument, I'm also adding a set argument,

30
00:03:25.060 --> 00:03:36.280
which is namely the set of elements that they have seen so far in our recursion over the list.

31
00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:41.800
So you have a function rem_dups, remove duplicates, it takes a list,

32
00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:46.800
it's defined recursively over the list, and takes the set, which is the set of elements that we've seen so far.

33
00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:52.180
And we say that, okay, if something, if we have an empty list,

34
00:03:52.180 --> 00:03:54.960
then we're trying the empty list because there's nothing to remove from the empty list.

35
00:03:55.280 --> 00:03:58.640
If we have an empty list, what we do is the following.

36
00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:04.790
We see that this X that we have at the front of our non-empty list,

37
00:04:05.190 --> 00:04:11.410
does it ever occur in the set of elements that we have seen so far?

38
00:04:12.030 --> 00:04:17.610
So in other words, is in XX, is that true or is that not true?

39
00:04:18.450 --> 00:04:27.460
If it is true, so if we have seen X before, then what we want to do is discard

40
00:04:27.460 --> 00:04:30.660
the X from our list.

41
00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:34.660
So we say we return remove duplicates of S and Xs only.

42
00:04:35.480 --> 00:04:41.340
If we have never seen X on the other hand, we don't discard X, so we return X cons REM duplicates,

43
00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:43.680
but what do we do here?

44
00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:49.520
We have that set of elements that we've seen until now and we add X to that set.

45
00:04:49.610 --> 00:04:56.750
And the second argument is Xs, which is the list without the front element.

46
00:04:57.670 --> 00:05:06.790
So this is an example of a function that you can define with using the set abstract data type.

47
00:05:06.790 --> 00:05:15.810
So this function doesn't really care about

48
00:05:17.410 --> 00:05:26.750
how the set ADT is implemented.

49
00:05:27.410 --> 00:05:33.110
All it cares about is that you have the operations from the set abstract data type,

50
00:05:33.190 --> 00:05:39.730
which are insertion and is in and empty, for instance, in the case of sets.

51
00:05:39.730 --> 00:05:46.770
And once you have those operations, you can specify the function rem_dups without really caring about how the set operations are implemented.

52
00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:53.710
And then in order for you to prove that rem_dups is correct,

53
00:05:54.630 --> 00:06:02.250
all what we do is we say, okay, we have here, if the invariant holds for the set that we have,

54
00:06:02.370 --> 00:06:09.950
the initial set that we have, then basically the rem_dups of S and Xs,

55
00:06:09.950 --> 00:06:19.120
so if you remove the duplicates from S and Xs, the set of elements that return from that

56
00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:25.460
is the same as the set of elements in the initial list Xs without S.

57
00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:31.980
So you remove the set of elements in S, all those elements, you remove them from the list Xs.

58
00:06:32.940 --> 00:06:42.140
And you also know that all the elements that are

59
00:06:42.680 --> 00:06:45.720
in the list returned by rem_dups as Xs

60
00:06:45.720 --> 00:06:52.840
are going to be pairwise distinguished, because anything that would have occurred twice or more

61
00:06:52.840 --> 00:06:59.000
would have already been seen in a previous call for the function rem_dups

62
00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:04.460
and would have accordingly been added here to the set S.

63
00:07:06.660 --> 00:07:13.440
So in other words, you end up with a list that doesn't have duplicates,

64
00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:22.520
and also you end up with a list that has all the elements in Xs here,

65
00:07:23.830 --> 00:07:28.610
minus the elements in the set of previously seen elements,

66
00:07:29.770 --> 00:07:34.910
because maybe you would initialize, for example, the set of seen elements with something that is not empty,

67
00:07:36.380 --> 00:07:41.000
then in that case you know that you would remove anything that was put in that set.

68
00:07:42.140 --> 00:07:50.020
And as a corollary, we know that if we provide that set of seen elements initially,

69
00:07:50.120 --> 00:07:53.880
if we initialize it with empty, so if that set is empty,

70
00:07:54.360 --> 00:08:00.160
we can then say, okay, rem_dups empty Xs is going to be the same as set of Xs,

71
00:08:00.160 --> 00:08:10.250
so in other words, the set of elements in that

72
00:08:10.250 --> 00:08:13.400
list is going to be the set of elements in the set Xs,

73
00:08:14.440 --> 00:08:20.520
because you only remove duplicates if you initialize the set of seen elements with empty.

74
00:08:20.520 --> 00:08:29.580
And similarly here, we know that the rem_dups of empty Xs will be

75
00:08:29.580 --> 00:08:31.480
a distinct list of elements.

76
00:08:32.650 --> 00:08:36.650
And both of those, this property is a consequence of that lemma,

77
00:08:37.110 --> 00:08:46.740
with the S initialized with the empty set, with the empty function from the ADT,

78
00:08:46.740 --> 00:08:52.400
and this thing is again resulting from that lemma with the same initialization of S.

79
00:08:56.530 --> 00:09:04.130
So this actually is the way,

80
00:09:04.310 --> 00:09:16.330
the general way, we prove, we use an element,

81
00:09:16.550 --> 00:09:19.470
we use an abstract type as a locale

82
00:09:19.470 --> 00:09:22.110
to implement an algorithm and to prove it correct.

83
00:09:22.250 --> 00:09:25.150
So we prove lemmas about it as if it's a normal algorithm,

84
00:09:25.610 --> 00:09:31.030
all of that is going to be within the context that fixes the assumed functions

85
00:09:31.030 --> 00:09:34.530
and fixes the specifications about those functions.

86
00:09:35.540 --> 00:09:44.080
And as an exercise, I would like you to prove lemma 1 in the same way we did all the proofs about lists

87
00:09:44.080 --> 00:09:45.880
that we've done in the weeks before,

88
00:09:46.280 --> 00:09:50.740
but I want you to prove it using the assumptions.

89
00:09:51.160 --> 00:09:59.560
So all the difference that we will have is that you will have the assumptions from the abstract data type,

90
00:10:00.360 --> 00:10:04.620
from the set abstract data type, we'll have all these assumptions accessible to you as if they are lemmas.

91
00:10:04.740 --> 00:10:11.660
So I want you to prove, to pause the video and prove lemma 1, this lemma here,

92
00:10:11.660 --> 00:10:16.960
I want you to prove that with those assumptions from the locale available to you.

